## TL;DR  

1. **Give the style a *second* sprite sheet** (the array form that MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0 understands).  
2. **Prefix the icon name with the sheet’s id** when you write `icon‑image`.  

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://tiles.provider.com/sprites/basemap" },
    { "id": "my-poi",   "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
  ],
  …
}
```

```js
// in the layer definition
{
  "id": "my-poi-layer",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "my-poi",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "my-poi:restaurant",   // ← prefix with the sheet id
    "icon-allow-overlap": true
  }
}
```

If you cannot edit the original style JSON, you can add the extra sheet **at runtime** with `map.addSprite()` after the `load` event and then use the same `my-poi:` prefix.

That’s all you need – no merging, no rebuilding, no extra `addImage()` calls.

---

## Why the icons never appeared

| What you did | What MapLibre expected | What actually happened |
|--------------|-----------------------|------------------------|
| `sprite: "https://tiles.provider.com/sprites/basemap"` (string) | **One** sprite sheet, id = `default`. | Only the provider’s PNG/JSON were loaded. |
| `icon-image: "restaurant"` (your POI name) | Look for `"restaurant"` in the *default* sheet. | Not found → the layer silently falls back to “no image”. No error is emitted because the style *did* load a sprite; the missing‑image warning is only fired when the **image id is requested but not present** in any loaded sheet. |
| You hosted a second sheet at `https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi` but never told the style about it. | No knowledge of that sheet → never requested. | Nothing to log. |

The fix is to make the style *aware* of the second sheet and to address the icons with the sheet’s id.

---

## Step‑by‑step guide

### 1. Verify you are on MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0

The multi‑sprite feature landed in **v3.0** (pull #1805).  
```js
console.log(map.version); // should be "3.x" or higher
```
If you are on an older version you must either upgrade, merge the sheets, or use `addImage()` for each icon.

### 2. Convert the `sprite` property to the array form

#### a) If you control the style JSON (recommended)

Replace the single string with an array of objects.  
*The provider’s sheet must keep the id `default`* so that all existing `icon-image` values that **don’t** have a prefix keep working unchanged.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://tiles.provider.com/sprites/basemap" },
    { "id": "my-poi",   "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
  ],
  …
}
```

**Important details**

| Rule | Why it matters |
|------|-----------------|
| **No file extension** in `url` | MapLibre appends `.json`, `.png`, and `@2x` automatically. |
| **Unique ids** | Duplicate ids cause a validation error (`all the sprites' ids must be unique`). |
| **`default` id** | Only this id can be omitted when referencing an image. |
| **CORS** | Both sprite PNG/JSON files must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` (or your origin). |
| **Same‑origin or CDN** | Hosting on a CDN, S3, GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc. works fine. Avoid `raw.githubusercontent.com` for production (rate‑limits, short cache, private‑repo 404s). |

#### b) If you cannot edit the style file (e.g. a third‑party style URL)

Add the extra sheet **after the style has loaded**:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // The provider’s sheet is already loaded as the default.
  map.addSprite('my-poi', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi')
    .then(() => console.log('my‑poi sprite added'))
    .catch(err => console.error('addSprite failed', err));
});
```

`addSprite` works exactly like the array form: it registers a new sheet with the given id and URL. The provider’s sheet stays the default because it was the original string value.

### 3. Reference your icons with the sheet id prefix

When you write a symbol layer’s `icon-image` (or any `*-pattern` property) you now have two ways to address an image:

| Image location | Syntax |
|----------------|--------|
| **Default sheet** (provider) | `"cafe"` |
| **Non‑default sheet** (your POI sheet) | `"my-poi:restaurant"` |
| **Both** (if you want to fallback) | `["coalesce", ["image", "my-poi:restaurant"], "cafe"]` (advanced) |

**Example layer**

```json
{
  "id": "poi-restaurants",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "poi",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "my-poi:restaurant",
    "icon-size": 0.9,
    "icon-allow-overlap": true
  }
}
```

### 4. Verify that the image is actually loaded

After the style and the extra sprite have finished loading you can ask the map:

```js
// after the `load` event (or after the promise from addSprite resolves)
if (map.hasImage('my-poi:restaurant')) {
  console.log('✅ restaurant icon is available');
} else {
  console.warn('❌ restaurant icon NOT found');
}
```

`map.listImages()` will list **all** loaded IDs, including the `my-poi:` prefix.

If `hasImage` returns `false`:

* Check the network tab – you should see `.../sprites/poi.json` and `.../sprites/poi.png` (and possibly `@2x` variants) with status 200.  
* Verify CORS headers on those files.  
* Open the JSON file and confirm the key `"restaurant"` exists (no extra prefix inside the JSON).  

If the network request never happens, the `url` you gave to `addSprite` or the style is wrong (missing trailing slash, extra `.json`, etc.).

### 5. (Optional) Fallback for older MapLibre versions

If you are stuck on a pre‑3.0 version, the only ways to use a second sheet are:

| Approach | How |
|----------|-----|
| **`addImage` for each icon** | Load the PNG/SVG yourself (`map.loadImage` or fetch → create `Image`) and call `map.addImage('restaurant', data)`. |
| **Merge the sheets** | Use `spritezero`, `spreet`, or `Martin` to combine the provider’s PNG/JSON with yours, then host the merged sheet as the single `sprite` URL. |
| **Upgrade** | Upgrading to v3+ is usually painless – just change the script tag or npm version. |

---

## Full working example (editable on CodePen / JSFiddle)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – two sprite sheets</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "sources": {
        "osm": { "type": "raster", "tiles": ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"], "tileSize": 256 }
      },
      "layers": [
        { "id": "osm", "type": "raster", "source": "osm" }
      ],
      // <-- two sprite sheets
      "sprite": [
        { "id": "default", "url": "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/sprites/ofm_f384/ofm" },
        { "id": "my-poi",   "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],
    zoom: 13
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // add a tiny GeoJSON source with two points
    map.addSource('my-poi', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [
          { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.77] }, properties: { name: 'cafe' } },
          { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.43, 37.78] }, properties: { name: 'restaurant' } }
        ]
      }
    });

    // Symbol layer that uses the custom sheet
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-poi-layer',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'my-poi',
      layout: {
        // note the prefix
        'icon-image': ['concat', 'my-poi:', ['get', 'name']],
        'icon-size': 0.8,
        'icon-allow-overlap': true
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi` with the real base URL of your PNG/JSON pair.*  
If you open the page and see the two points with the custom icons, the setup works.

