## Common pitfalls & how to spot them

| Symptom | Likely cause | How to debug |
|---------|--------------|--------------|
| **No icon, no console warning** | The extra sheet never loaded (wrong URL, missing CORS, or using a pre‑3.0 MapLibre). | Open DevTools → Network → look for `.../sprites/poi.json` and `.../sprites/poi.png`. Check status 200 and `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin`. |
| **`Image "<id>" could not be loaded`** | The ID you referenced does not exist **in any loaded sheet** (typo, missing prefix). | `map.listImages()` → does the exact string appear? |
| **Icons appear but are all the same (e.g., all “cafe”)** | You forgot the prefix, so MapLibre falls back to the default sheet’s “cafe”. | Verify the layer’s `icon-image` value in the style inspector (or `console.log` the expression result). |
| **`addSprite` promise rejects** | URL points to a JSON file *with* an extension (`.json`) → MapLibre tries `...json.json`. | Ensure the URL ends **without** `.json` or `.png`. |
| **CORS error** | Sprite PNG/JSON served without `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin`. | Add the header on your static host (e.g., S3 bucket policy, Netlify `_headers`, Vercel `vercel.json`). |
| **Only the `@2x` version loads** | You are on a high‑DPI screen and the `@2x` PNG is missing. | Provide both `sprite.png` and `sprite@2x.png` (and matching JSON). |

---

## Recap checklist

1. **Upgrade** to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0 (if needed).  
2. **Add a second sprite sheet**  
   * either by editing the style’s `sprite` array, **or** by calling `map.addSprite('my-poi', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi')` after `load`.  
3. **Prefix** every custom icon name with the sheet id (`my-poi:`).  
4. **Confirm** the sheet loads (network

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + category icons are stored as **SVG files** on your own domain (`https://my‑site.com/icons/<name>.svg`).  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains ~5 000 point features, each with a string property `category` that matches one of the SVG file names (e.g. `"cafe"`, `"park"` …).  
* The icons are **rasterised at run‑time** with `Map.addImage()`.  
* Collision handling is left to the style engine (`icon‑allow‑overlap: false` – the default) so that, when you are zoomed out, icons that would overlap are automatically hidden.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 CSS & JS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Configuration – change these to match your own setup
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN = '';               // not needed for pure MapLibre, keep empty
const MAP_STYLE = 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json'; // any style you like
const ICON_BASE_URL = 'https://my-site.com/icons/'; // trailing slash
const ICON_NAMES = [
  // list every category name that exists as an SVG file
  'cafe', 'park', 'restaurant', 'library', 'museum', 'school',
  'hospital', 'bank', 'pharmacy', 'post_office', 'hotel', 'bar',
  // … add the rest (≈40 total) …
];
const GEOJSON_URL = 'https://my-site.com/data/venues.geojson';
/* -------------------------------------------------------------- */

(async () => {
  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     3️⃣  Initialise the map
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: MAP_STYLE,
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],   // San Francisco – change as you wish
    zoom: 12,
    // optional: set maxZoom/minZoom if you want tighter control
  });

  // Wait for the style to be fully loaded before we start adding images
  await new Promise(resolve => map.once('load', resolve));

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     4️⃣  Helper – load an SVG, rasterise it into an Image object
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  function loadSvgAsImage(url, size = 24) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const img = new Image(size, size);
      img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';          // required for cross‑origin SVGs
      img.onload = () => resolve(img);
      img.onerror = e => reject(new Error(`Failed to load SVG ${url}`));
      img.src = url;
    });
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     5️⃣  Load *all* SVG icons and register them with the map
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  const iconPromises = ICON_NAMES.map(async name => {
    const url = `${ICON_BASE_URL}${name}.svg`;
    const img = await loadSvgAsImage(url, 24);   // 24 px square – adjust if you like
    // Guard against duplicate IDs (e.g. if the style already ships a “cafe” icon)
    if (!map.hasImage(name)) {
      map.addImage(name, img, { sdf: false });   // ordinary raster image
    }
  });

  // Wait until every icon is in the style
  await Promise.all(iconPromises);
  console.log('All icons loaded and added to the style');

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     6️⃣  Add the GeoJSON source (5 000 points)
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: GEOJSON_URL,
    // optional: enable clustering if you want a different visual at very low zooms
    // cluster: true,
    // clusterRadius: 50
  });

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     7️⃣  Add the symbol layer that uses the icons
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    // The icon name comes directly from the feature property `category`
    layout: {
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],   // e.g. "cafe", "park", …
      'icon-size': 1,                      // scale factor – tweak if icons look too big/small
      // Collision handling (default is false → icons hide on overlap)
      // You can fine‑tune the priority with `symbol-sort-key` if you have a
      // secondary ranking (e.g. popularity). Here we just let the engine decide.
      // 'symbol-sort-key': ['get', 'rank']   // optional
    },
    paint: {
      // Optional visual tweaks
      // 'icon-color': '#2a2a2a',   // works only on SDF icons – ignored for normal SVGs
      // 'icon-opacity': 0.9
    }
  });

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     8️⃣  (Optional) Show a popup with the venue name on click
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ closeButton: false, closeOnClick: false });

  map.on('click', 'venue-icons', e => {
    const feature = e.features[0];
    const coordinates = feature.geometry.coordinates.slice();
    const name = feature.properties.name || 'Unnamed venue';
    const category = feature.properties.category;

    popup.setLngLat(coordinates)
         .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>Category: ${category}`)
         .addTo(map);
  });

  // Change the cursor to a pointer when hovering a venue
  map.on('mouseenter', 'venue-icons', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
  map.on('mouseleave', 'venue-icons', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');

})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## How the pieces fit together

| Step | What happens | Why it matters for your use‑case |
|------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| **3️⃣ Initialise the map** | `map.once('load')` guarantees the style (including any default sprite) is ready before we start adding our own images. | Prevents the “styleimagemissing” warning that would appear if we tried to add images too early. |
| **4️⃣ loadSvgAsImage()** | Creates an `HTMLImageElement` from the remote SVG (cross‑origin enabled) and resolves when the rasterisation finishes. | `Map.addImage()` only accepts raster images (PNG/WebP/JPEG) or an `Image` element; this is the bridge for SVGs. |
| **5️⃣ addImage() for every icon** | Each SVG is registered under the exact string that appears in the GeoJSON `category` property. `hasImage()` avoids duplicate‑ID errors if the base style already ships an icon with the same name. | The style now knows about **all 40+ icons**; the layer can reference them directly via `['get','category']`. |
| **6️⃣ GeoJSON source** | Loads the 5 000 venue points in one request (or you could inline the data). | Keeps the data separate from the style, allowing you to update it without rebuilding the sprite. |
| **7️⃣ symbol layer** | `icon-image` is an **expression** that reads the feature’s `category`. Collision handling is left at the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`), so when you zoom out the engine hides overlapping icons automatically. | Exactly what you asked for: each venue shows its own icon, and the icons “don’t pile up” at low zooms. |
| **8️⃣ optional popup** | Demonstrates how to interact with the points; not required for the core task. | Shows that the icons are still regular MapLibre features you can query. |

---

## Tweaking collision behaviour (optional)

If you find that too many icons disappear when you are moderately zoomed out, you can:

```js
layout: {
  'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],
  'icon-allow-overlap': true,          // draw every icon, ignore collisions
  // or:
  'icon-ignore-placement': true,       // same effect, newer name
  // or give priority to certain venues:
  'symbol-sort-key': ['get', 'popularity']   // higher value → drawn on top
}
```

But **the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) already does exactly what you described** – hide icons that would overlap, keeping the map readable.

---

## Things to double‑check in your own deployment

1. **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or at least your map domain). Otherwise `new Image()` will throw a security error.
2. **SVG size** – The `new Image(width, height)` call determines the rasterised pixel size. If you need different sizes per category, you can store that information in a lookup table and call `addImage(name, img, { pixelRatio: … })` accordingly.
3. **Cache** – Browsers will cache the SVGs automatically; the `Promise.all()` pattern ensures we only request each file once.
4. **Performance** – 5 000 points is well within the limits of a single symbol layer. If you ever go to > 50 000 points, consider **clustering** (`cluster: true` in the source) or **vector‑tile** sources.

That’s it! Drop the HTML into a file, adjust the three configuration constants (`ICON_BASE_URL`, `ICON_NAMES`, `GEOJSON_URL`), and you’ll have a fully functional MapLibre map with 5 000 venues each showing its own SVG‑derived icon, with automatic collision avoidance. Happy mapping!

## TL;DR  

*The current heuristic (`text‑anchor` missing **and** `symbol‑placement === "point"`) is **not reliable** – it will fire on any ordinary point‑symbol layer (POI icons, place‑name icons, custom markers, etc.) and will miss many real shield layers that don’t use those exact defaults.*  

**A better “shield‑detector** should look at the *combination* of properties that are unique to the way the OSM‑Bright / OSM‑Liberty / OpenMapTiles styles build shields:  

| What to test | Why it works | Typical value in a shield layer |
|--------------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| `layer.type === "symbol"` | Shields are always symbol layers | `"symbol"` |
| `layout["text-field"]` **exists** and contains a `{ref}` placeholder (or a plain `{ref}` string) | The route number is rendered as text, not as an icon | `"{ref}"` |
| `layout["icon-image"]` **exists** and is an **expression** that contains a placeholder for the network or the ref‑length (`{network}`, `{ref_length}`, `road_{ref_length}`, etc.) | The badge behind the number comes from the sprite; the placeholder is what makes the badge size depend on the length of the ref | `["concat", ["get", "network"], "_", ["get", "ref_length"]]` or the string template `"{network}_{ref_length}"` |
| (`layout["icon-text-fit"] !== "none"` **or** `layout["icon-image"]` contains a placeholder) – i.e. **either** a *stretchable* badge **or** a *pre‑drawn* badge | There are two families of shields (stretchable vs. pre‑drawn). Detecting either covers both. | `"both"` / `"width"` **or** missing (pre‑drawn) |
| `layout["text-font"]` **exists** (usually a bold font) | Shields always have a font for the ref number; most other point‑symbol layers either omit `text-font` or use a generic label font. | `["Open Sans Bold","Arial Unicode MS Regular"]` |
| `layout["icon-allow-overlap"] === true` **or** `layout["icon-ignore-placement"] === true` (optional but common) | Shields are drawn on top of the line and should never be culled by collision detection. | `true` |
| `layout["symbol-placement"]` is **either** `"point"` **or** a zoom‑function that resolves to `"point"` | Some styles use a function that switches between `"point"` and `"line"`; you need to evaluate the function at a typical zoom (e.g. 12) to see if it resolves to `"point"`. | `"point"` or `["step", ["zoom"], "point", 7, "line", 8, "line"]` |
| **Optional sanity check** – the source‑layer is something like `transportation_name`, `road_name`, or `shield` (if you know the source schema). | Shields are usually drawn from the *name* layer of the transportation tileset. | `"transportation_name"` |

If **all** of the *required* conditions are met (text‑field with `{ref}` **and** an icon‑image that references a placeholder), you can safely label the layer as a *shield*.

---

## Why the old heuristic fails

```js
if (layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
    layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
    // flag as shield
}
```

| Problem | Example of false‑positive | Example of false‑negative |
|---------|---------------------------|---------------------------|
| `text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` when omitted, so many point‑symbol layers have it **undefined**. | A POI layer that only shows an icon (`cafe`, `park`) – no `text-anchor` → flagged as shield. | A shield layer that explicitly sets `text-anchor: "center"` (some styles do) → not flagged. |
| `symbol-placement` defaults to `"point"` for point sources, but many non‑shield layers also use the default. | Place‑name layers that render a label with an optional icon (`place-label`) – also `symbol-placement: "point"` → flagged. | A shield layer that uses a **function** (`["step", ["zoom"], "point", 7, "line"]`) → the raw value is an array, not the string `"point"` → not flagged. |
| No check that the layer actually **has a text field** or **icon image** that looks like a shield. | A layer that only draws an icon (e.g., `cafe`) – will be mis‑identified. | A shield layer that uses a *stretchable* badge (`icon-text-fit: "both"`) but does not set `icon-image` to a placeholder (the badge is a single generic image) – will be missed. |

In short, the heuristic is **too generic** (matches any point‑symbol) and **too narrow** (misses shields that don’t use the exact defaults).

---

## A concrete detection function (ESM / TypeScript‑friendly)

Below is a ready‑to‑paste helper you can drop into a style‑audit script. It works with both **static values** and **expressions** (including step/lookup functions) by *evaluating* the expression at a representative zoom (default 12). Feel free to tweak the zoom or the list of placeholder tokens.

```ts
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route‑shield layer.
 *
 * @param layer   The style layer object (already parsed from the style JSON)
 * @param zoom    Zoom level used to evaluate zoom‑functions (default 12)
 */
export function isShieldLayer(layer: any, zoom = 12): boolean {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== "symbol") return false;

  const layout = layer.layout ?? {};

  // 2️⃣ Must have a text field that references the route ref
  const textField = layout["text-field"];
  if (!textField) return false;
  // text-field can be a string or an expression; we just need to see {ref}
  const hasRefPlaceholder =
    typeof textField === "string"
      ? textField.includes("{ref}")
      : // expression – look for a literal string containing {ref}
        JSON.stringify(textField).includes("{ref}");
  if (!hasRefPlaceholder) return false;

  // 3️⃣ Must have an icon‑image that references a placeholder (network or ref_length)
  const iconImage = layout["icon-image"];
  if (!iconImage) return false;

  const placeholderTokens = ["{network}", "{ref_length}", "road_", "us-"];
  const iconLooksLikeBadge = (value: any): boolean => {
    if (typeof value === "string") {
      return placeholderTokens.some(tok => value.includes(tok));
    }
    // expression – stringify and search for the tokens
    const str = JSON.stringify(value);
    return placeholderTokens.some(tok => str.includes(tok));
  };
  if (!iconLooksLikeBadge(iconImage)) return false;

  // 4️⃣ Either a stretchable badge (icon-text-fit !== "none") OR a pre‑drawn badge (placeholder above)
  const iconTextFit = layout["icon-text-fit"];
  const isStretchable = iconTextFit && iconTextFit !== "none";

  // If we got here we already know there is a placeholder, so we accept both families.
  // (If you want to be stricter you can require one of the two.)
  // 5️⃣ Symbol‑placement must resolve to "point" (or be omitted → defaults to point)
  const placement = layout["symbol-placement"];
  const resolvesToPoint = (() => {
    if (placement === undefined) return true; // default
    if (typeof placement === "string") return placement === "point";
    // expression – evaluate at the supplied zoom
    // Very simple evaluator: only handles step/lookup that return a string.
    // For a full evaluator you could import @maplibre/maplibre-gl-style-spec's evaluate.
    try {
      // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-var-requires
      const { evaluate } = require("@maplibre/maplibre-gl-style-spec");
      const result = evaluate(placement, { zoom });
      return result === "point";
    } catch {
      // If we cannot evaluate, be conservative and assume it might be point.
      return false;
    }
  })();

  if (!resolvesToPoint) return false;

  // 6️⃣ (Optional) Check that a bold font is used – shields usually set a font.
  const textFont = layout["text-font"];
  if (textFont && Array.isArray(textFont)) {
    const hasBold = textFont.some((f: string) => /bold/i.test(f));
    if (!hasBold) return false;
  }

  // 7️⃣ (Optional) Overlap flags – shields normally ignore placement.
  const allowOverlap = layout["icon-allow-overlap"] === true ||
                       layout["icon-ignore-placement"] === true;
  // Not a hard requirement; many styles omit it.

  // If we passed all required checks, we consider it a shield.
  return true;
}
```

### How the function works

| Step | What it checks | What it catches |
|------|----------------|-----------------|
| **1** | `type === "symbol"` | Guarantees we’re only looking at symbol layers. |
| **2** | `text-field` contains `{ref}` | Shields always render the route number as text. |
| **3** | `icon-image` contains a placeholder (`{network}`, `{ref_length}`, `road_`, `us-`) | Distinguishes badge images from generic POI icons (`cafe`, `park`). |
| **4** | Accepts both *stretchable* (`icon-text-fit !== "none"`) **or** *pre‑drawn* badge (placeholder). | Covers both families of shields. |
| **5** | `symbol-placement` resolves to `"point"` (or defaults). | Handles the common function form (`["step", ["zoom"], "point", 7, "line"]`). |
| **6** (optional) | `text-font` includes a bold font. | Filters out layers that use a generic label font. |
| **7** (optional) | `icon-allow-overlap` / `icon-ignore-placement` true. | Shields usually ignore collisions; not required but useful for extra confidence. |

You can drop steps 6‑7 if you want a looser detection; steps 2‑4 are the **minimum** that reliably separate shields from ordinary point‑symbol layers.

---

## Real‑world examples

| Style | Layer ID | `icon-image` (raw) | `text-field` | `icon-text-fit` | Result |
|-------|----------|-------------------|--------------|----------------|--------|
| **OSM‑Bright** | `highway-shield-us-interstate` | `"{network}_{ref_length}"` | `"{ref}"` | *none* (pre‑drawn) | **Shield** |
| **OSM‑Bright** | `highway-shield-us-interstate-stretch` | `"us-interstate"` (single image) | `"{ref}"` | `"both"` (stretchable) | **Shield** |
| **OSM‑Bright** | `poi-label` | *none* (text only) | `"{name}"` | *none* | **Not a shield** |
| **OSM‑Bright** | `cafe` | `"cafe"` | *none* | *none* | **Not a shield** |
| **Custom style** | `my-place-names` | `"place"` | `"{name}"` | *none* | **Not a shield** |
| **Custom style** | `my-shields` | `["concat", ["get","network"], "_", ["get","ref_length"]]` | `"{ref}"` | *none* | **Shield** |

Notice how the **presence of `{ref}` in `text-field`** and a **placeholder in `icon-image`** is the decisive factor.

---

## Quick checklist for a manual audit

If you’re just eyeballing a style file, look for the following **signature pattern**:

1. `type: "symbol"`  
2. `layout.text-field` contains `{ref}` (or a literal reference to a property that holds the route number).  
3. `layout.icon-image` is **not** a static name like `"

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste** example that shows:

1. **Create a MapLibre GL JS map** (any style you like).  
2. **Add a draggable `Marker`** (the “pin”) at a fixed longitude‑latitude.  
3. **Attach a `Popup`** that opens when the user clicks the pin.  
4. **Update the popup text** (or any other UI) when the pin is dragged to a new location.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Draggable pin with popup – MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
    rel="stylesheet"
  />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',                     // HTML element id
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you own
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],             // initial view (lon, lat)
    zoom: 13
  });

  // Wait until the style is fully loaded before adding any UI.
  map.on('load', () => {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Create the popup (but don’t open it yet)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
      offset: 25,               // distance from the marker tip
      closeButton: true,
      closeOnClick: false      // keep it open when the map is clicked
    }).setHTML('<strong>Drag me!</strong><br/>Lng/Lat: <span id="coords"></span>');

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Create the draggable marker
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
      draggable: true,          // <‑‑ makes the pin draggable
      color: '#ff0000'          // optional – change the default blue pin
    })
      .setLngLat([-122.42, 37.78])   // start position
      .setPopup(popup)               // attach the popup to the marker
      .addTo(map);                   // finally add it to the map

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Helper: keep the popup text in sync with the pin
    // -------------------------------------------------
    function updatePopupContent(lngLat) {
      const el = document.getElementById('coords');
      if (el) {
        el.textContent = `${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}, ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`;
      }
    }

    // Initialise the text the first time
    updatePopupContent(marker.getLngLat());

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ Open the popup when the user clicks the pin
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // `setPopup()` already wires the click → open behaviour,
    // but you can also force it manually if you need custom logic:
    // marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', () => marker.togglePopup());

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣ Listen for drag events and update the popup
    // -------------------------------------------------
    marker.on('dragend', () => {
      const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();   // new position after drag
      updatePopupContent(lngLat);
      // If you want the popup to stay open after dragging:
      marker.togglePopup();   // close → open (or just call .setPopup(popup) again)
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

---

## How the pieces fit together

| Piece | What it does | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **`new maplibregl.Map({...})`** | Boots the map canvas and loads the style. | You need a map before you can add any UI. |
| **`new maplibregl.Popup({...})`** | Creates a floating HTML box that can be attached to a marker. | Popups are the standard way to show extra info on click. |
| **`new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })`** | Instantiates a pin that can be moved by the user. | `draggable:true` turns the marker into a “pin”. |
| **`.setLngLat([lon, lat])`** | Places the marker at a known coordinate. | Gives the pin its initial location. |
| **`.setPopup(popup)`** | Binds the popup to the marker. | Clicking the marker automatically opens the popup (default behaviour). |
| **`.addTo(map)`** | Renders the marker on the map. | Without this the marker never appears. |
| **`marker.on('dragend', …)`** | Fires after the user finishes dragging. | Lets you react (e.g., update the popup text, store the new coordinate, request data, etc.). |
| **`marker.togglePopup()`** (optional) | Manually open/close the popup. | Useful if you want the popup to stay open after a drag or to close it programmatically. |

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Popup never opens on click** | You called `setPopup()` *after* the marker was already added, but you also set `closeOnClick:true` on the popup **and** the map’s `click` event is swallowing the click. | Keep `closeOnClick:false` on the popup (or omit it – the default is `true` but the marker still opens it). If you have a global `map.on('click', …)` that calls `popup.remove()`, make sure you check `e.originalEvent.target` to ignore clicks on the marker element. |
| **Marker jumps to a different place after dragging** | The map’s `dragPan` or `dragRotate` is interfering with the marker’s drag events (rare, but can happen when you have custom `pointer-events` CSS). | Ensure the marker element has `pointer-events: auto` (the default). |
| **`loadImage` or `addImage` errors** (if you tried to use a custom SVG instead of a built‑in marker) | SVGs need to be rasterised before `addImage`. | Use the `Image`‑element technique shown in the **maplibre‑sprites‑icons** skill, or stick with the built‑in `Marker` which already uses a PNG sprite. |
| **CORS errors when loading a custom style or sprite** | The style’s `sprite` URL (or the style JSON itself) is on a domain that doesn’t send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*`. | Host the style/sprite on the same origin as your page, or enable CORS on the server (or use a CDN that adds the header). |
| **Marker disappears at high zoom levels** | The default marker image is a 24 px PNG; at very high DPI (`devicePixelRatio > 2`) the map may request a `@2x` sprite that doesn’t exist. | Provide a `@2x` version of the sprite (or use the built‑in marker which already ships with both resolutions). |

---

## Extending the example

| Goal | How to do it |
|------|--------------|
| **Persist the new coordinate** (e.g., send to a server) | Inside `marker.on('dragend', …)` call `fetch('/save', {method:'POST', body: JSON.stringify(marker.getLngLat())})`. |
| **Show a different icon while dragging** | Listen to `dragstart` / `dragend` and call `marker.getElement().classList.add('dragging')` and style that class with CSS (e.g., change opacity). |
| **Add a custom SVG pin** | Use `map.loadImage()` (PNG/WebP) **or** rasterise an `<svg>` into an `Image` element, then `map.addImage('my-pin', img, { sdf: false })` and create a `Marker` with `{ element: document.createElement('div') }` that sets `background-image: url('my-pin')`. See the **maplibre‑sprites‑icons** skill for the full workflow. |
| **Multiple draggable pins** | Keep an array of `Marker` objects, each with its own `dragend` listener. |
| **Show the popup on hover instead of click** | Replace `marker.setPopup(popup)` with `marker.getElement().addEventListener('mouseenter', () => popup.addTo(map));` and close on `mouseleave`. |

---

### TL;DR (one‑liner)

```js
new maplibregl.Marker({draggable:true})
  .setLngLat([lon, lat])
  .setPopup(new maplibregl.Popup().setHTML('Hello!'))
  .addTo(map);
```

That single chain creates a draggable pin, attaches a popup, and the popup opens automatically when the user clicks the pin. The longer example above shows how to keep the popup content in sync with the pin’s position and how to handle the drag‑end event. Happy mapping!
